  "body": {
    "id": "prop:rare-history-stress-test",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "Fix any enrollment position \\((b,i)\\), schedule \\(Y\\), and arm \\(z\\) such that \\(p_{bi}(z\\mid H_{bi})>0\\) holds \\(P^O_{\\mathcal D,Y}\\)-almost surely, and fix any predictable event \\(E\\in H_{bi}\\).  The event's contribution to the average inverse-propensity factor is exactly \n\\(\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal D,Y}[\\mathbf 1_E\\{1-p_{bi}(z\\mid H_{bi})\\}/p_{bi}(z\\mid H_{bi})]\\).  If \\(P^O_{\\mathcal D,Y}(E)\\le\\epsilon\\) and \\(p_{bi}(z\\mid H_{bi})\\ge\\delta\\) on \\(E\\), this contribution is at most \\(\\epsilon(1-\\delta)/\\delta\\).  Thus a rare \\(\\epsilon\\)-probability branch is charged through its assignment probability rather than by the worst-path factor \\(1/\\delta\\) alone.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "Y_bi",
      "E_rare",
      "H_bi",
      "D_design",
      "p_bi",
      "P_obs_Y",
      "epsilon",
      "delta"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:sequential-randomization",
      "ass:propensity-floor",
      "def:average-design-scale"
    ],
    "justification": "This calculation is the required low-propensity audit and prevents an information lower bound from treating negligible histories as certain.",
    "gap": "Worst-path martingale bounds are valid for coverage but do not by themselves characterize worst-case expected length under adaptive randomization.",
    "consumer": "It tells trial software how rare adaptive branches enter the borrowing penalty and guards against severe overconservatism.",
    "proof_tex": "Let \\(p=p_{bi}(z\\mid H_{bi})\\).  The added positivity condition makes the displayed random variable well defined.  Its contribution on \\(E\\) is, by restriction of the expectation under the actual design law,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal D,Y}\\left[\\mathbf 1_E\\frac{1-p}{p}\\right].\n\\]\nOn \\(E\\), the hypothesis \\(p\\ge\\delta>0\\) and monotonicity of \\(x\\mapsto x^{-1}-1\\) on \\((0,1]\\) give\n\\[\n0\\le\\frac{1-p}{p}\\le\\frac{1-\\delta}{\\delta}.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal D,Y}\\left[\\mathbf 1_E\\frac{1-p}{p}\\right]\n\\le \\frac{1-\\delta}{\\delta}P^O_{\\mathcal D,Y}(E)\n\\le\\epsilon\\frac{1-\\delta}{\\delta}.\n\\]\nThis is an assignment-law average: the factor contributed by the branch is multiplied by its actual probability.  Assumption \\(\\mathrm{ass:propensity\\text{-}floor}\\) guarantees the positivity premise, with \\(p\\ge p_{\\min}\\), whenever the fixed arm is available.",
